\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It was experimentally demonstrated that a dense electron-hole plasma is
 generated upon femtosecond laser irradiation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Sokolowski-Tinten2000"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A quantum description of silicon absorption upon laser irradiation requires
 a proper description of the laser-excited electron-hole pairs 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Reining2002,Kim2002,Marini2003"

\end_inset

, i.e.
 of excitons.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Such transient excitation leads to the change of refractive index as a
 function of time and laser energy and can lead to excitation of several
 processes such as triggering chemical reaction, non-thermal phase transitions,
 and surface plasmon polariton excitation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Derrien2013,Sato2014a"

\end_inset

.
 Making use of multi-photonic absorption also enabled outstanding applications
 such as 2-photon polymerization [REF], and multiphoton microscopy [REF].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First theoretical description of non-linear absorption in matter was successful
 but are nowadays not sufficient to address the needs for describing laser-induc
ed band gap energy levels, non-parabolic band structure, or the wide spectrum
 of ultrashort laser pulses.
 Nowadays, strong hypothesis such as monochromaticity of waves, parabolic
 band gap approximation, and direct interband transitions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Keldysh1965,Garcia2006"

\end_inset

 could not be easily removed.
 Therefore, calculating the non-linear excitation transition rates in matter
 remain a puzzling problem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition, making use of several laser pulses together lead to give more
 control over laser ablation , nanostructure formation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Derrien2013a,Hoehm2013a,Hoehm2015,Hoehm2015b"

\end_inset

, high harmonic generation and [see results from Les Houches workshop].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In view of bridging large scale simulations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Bulgakova2013,Bulgakova2014,Buschlinger2014"

\end_inset

 with first principle calculations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Flick2015a,Ruggenthaler2014"

\end_inset

, this paper propose a quantum simulation of the non-linear absorption in
 indirect band gap material with the example of silicon, based on the method
 of TD-DFT.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Calculation method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the quantum modeling world, the linear absorption in bulk is typically
 resolved using the meta-Generalized Gradient Approximation (MGGA) qualitatively
 well reproducing the experimentally measured absorption spectrum for Si
 and Ge 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Nazarov2011"

\end_inset

.
 A full picture of the photo-emission from atoms and molecules also came
 to be available 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "DeGiovannini2012"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, it appears that linear absorption can be calculated using methods
 like MGGA, or Kohn-Sham approach with exchange-correlation derivated from
 Bethe-Salpeter equation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Difficulty appears to be linked with the description of excitons bounded
 on large computational cell, and with non-linear excitation (intense fields).
 So far, the case of gas phase ionization was well studied (so called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

above threshold ionization
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) [
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Keldysh1965"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Chin1988"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Ruggenthaler2008,Popruzhenko2008a"

\end_inset

].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Today the difficulty remains to calculate excitation rates in correlated
 matter upon intense laser light.
 Such calculation started for small metallic systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Otobe2004,Shiratori2005,Otobe2008,Otobe2009,Tong2015"

\end_inset

.
 In addition, the effect of intense constant field got recently studied
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Otobe2016"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper, we will show results of quantum simulations made on Si, from
 the most basic case to the most advanced.
 This will support the development of my work in MPSD.
 We will select the interesting ones before publication.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Preparation of the laser pulses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Lasers in quantum simulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the scale of the sample is of few atomic layers, the local electric field
 of the laser can be approximated by its simple temporal variation.
 Simulations are resolved in a space-time-dependent approach, hence the
 dispersion of the laser pulse is natively taken into account.
 For short pulse durations, the spectrum of the pulses becomes wider.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If using QED-DFT 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Flick2015a"

\end_inset

, some modulations of the electric field response may be observed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Temporal evolution of bi-color pulses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Experimental techniques
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the experiments, two configurations are possible.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
(i) We can make use of one laser source, and place a BBO crystal in the
 optical path, then focusing both pulses using the same lens, in the most
 ideal case.
 In this case, the two pulses will have blocked relative phase 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP_{2}-CEP_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
(ii) Second possibility is to use two different lasers, which may then have
 two different phases 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP_{2}$
\end_inset

, even maybe time-varying relative phases.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now draw a general model which takes into account all possible configurations.
 At the atomic scale, the spatial variation of the pulse envelope can be
 neglected.
 Hence, the temporal envelope of the pulse can be defined by the general
 bi-color pulse expression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{E}(t)=\boldsymbol{E}_{\lambda_{1}}(t)+\boldsymbol{E}_{\lambda_{2}}(t,\Delta t).
\]

\end_inset

Each pulse can be developed in
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{E}_{\lambda_{1}}=\boldsymbol{E}_{0}^{(1)}\sin\left(\omega_{1}t+CEP_{1}\right)\cdot\exp\left[-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{t}{\sigma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}\right)^{2}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{E}_{\lambda_{2}}=\boldsymbol{E}_{0}^{(2)}\sin\left(\omega_{2}t+CEP_{2}\right)\cdot\exp\left[-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{t-\Delta t}{\sigma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\right)^{2}\right]
\]

\end_inset

These two pulses defines a space with 8 dimensions to be investigated: 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}^{(1)}$
\end_inset

,  
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

, but also the orientation 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}$
\end_inset

 of the electric field in real space (polarization) parallel to surface
 (
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

) state for each pulse.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Effect of the second wavelength
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular, it is predicted that effect of the relative 
\begin_inset Formula $CEP$
\end_inset

 for the interfered beams will strongly influence the bicolor transition
 probability.
 
