\begin_layout Subsection
Preparation of the sample
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We generate a silicon slab with using periodic boundary conditions along
 the surface, and absorbing boundary conditions in the direction normal
 to the surface.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Which orientation to choose ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the context of nanostructure formation, the effects of crystal orientation
 were only slightly investigated.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Crawford2010,Museur,Sedao2014"

\end_inset

 It was shown that crystal orientation should not play a role in the periodic
 nanostructures of electrodynamic origin.
 However, it may play a role in the formation thermodynamically formed periodic
 structures.
 As Si has a diamond-cubic crystalline structure, we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Si}(100)$
\end_inset

, and Si
\begin_inset Formula $(111)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
2d-periodic Si slab
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In view of studying the effect of the surface, we build a 2D-periodic Si
 lattice with periodic boundary conditions in directions X and Y.
 
