\begin_layout Standard
As we would like to observe the evolution of the wavefunction density 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

 in the sample, we generate two types of samples.
 Slab 1 is the smallest sample where a large grid of k-points allows for
 a good convergence of electronic level calculation.
 Slab 2 is a second slab describing the same material, where the number
 of atoms is increased in view of maximizing the spatially resolved information.
 Such procedure does not affect the integrity of calculation, as the whole
 physical information is contained into the Kohn-Sham wavefunction densities
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The size of Slab 2 was increased until exhibiting the same energy per atom
 as Slab 1.
 As soon as the sample was thick enough, overlapping between surface induced
 energy levels and bulk energy levels was suppressed, leading to convergence
 of the sample to the same total energy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Procedure for building a minimal slab (SLAB 1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1.
 Calculate the energy per atom, via making a bulk calculation.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{at}=\frac{E_{tot}}{N_{at}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2.
 Increase slab depth until convergence of surface energy (see Eq.
 (2) in paper Beschtedt ).
 Means that both surface are not interacting (wavefunctions are not overlapping
 any longer).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
3.
 Once this slab (minimal number of atoms in the plane) is built, we start
 calculating the k-points to calculate band structure.
 Find the required number of k points so that electronic total energy per
 atom does not change.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
4.
 Once it is obtained, we found the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{e}$
\end_inset

 of the ground state (
\begin_inset Formula $T=0$
\end_inset

 K).
 The whole observables are available from this knowledge (DFT).
\end_layout

