\begin_layout Subsubsection
Building a large SLAB 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1.
 From SLAB1 > Step 2, we have a column which should be repeated in X and
 Y, using only 1 k-point.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2.
 We add atoms in X and Y directions (same number in both directions) until
 total energy is converged, and equal to SLAB 1 > Step 4 energy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
3.
 Then we know that we have built the proper ground state for the supercell
 of Si.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Simulation results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are basically interested in accessing to the temporal density evolution
 of electrons 
\emph on
in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{k}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial N_{\boldsymbol{k}}}{\partial t}
\]

\end_inset

promoted from the valence bands to the conduction band of Si as described
 by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t)=\int\psi_{k}^{\dagger}(\boldsymbol{r},t)\,\psi_{k}(\boldsymbol{r},t)\, d\boldsymbol{r}.
\]

\end_inset

As shown recently [REF quantum simulation of linear excitation], the linear
 processes are exciting electrons towards many wave-vectors of the conduction
 band.
 The difficulty is to restrict the number of carriers to the conduction
 band of Si, which may also evolve in time.
 In particular, at high laser intensity, ps-melting 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Siwick2003"

\end_inset

 and non-thermal melting 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Rousse2001,Sundaram2002"

\end_inset

 can occur, where the band gap undergoes a collapsing effect.
 
\end_layout

