Coordinates of the atom in the 3d-diamond Si lattice are given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{cases}
\left(0,0,0\right) & \left(\frac{a}{2},0,\frac{a}{2}\right)\\
\left(\frac{3a}{4},\frac{3a}{4},\frac{a}{4}\right) & \left(0,\frac{a}{2},\frac{a}{2}\right)\\
\left(\frac{3a}{4},\frac{a}{4},\frac{3a}{4}\right) & \left(\frac{a}{2},\frac{a}{2},0\right)\\
\left(\frac{a}{4},\frac{3a}{4},\frac{3a}{4}\right) & \left(\frac{a}{4},\frac{a}{4},\frac{a}{4}\right)
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a\sim5.43\ \textrm{Å}$
\end_inset

using an orthonormal basis.
 However, it can be expressed with less atoms using a parallelepipedic basis
 [Fig 
